            <div xml:id="d667">

                <pb facs="0708" n="604" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg604"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.00/879: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Donald</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-06-18T13:00:00-06:00">June 18, 1932—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>50. Your circular June 17, 6 p.m. My impression of reaction in Guatemala to
                    Martínez manifesto is that it is felt that Martínez has put something over on
                    the United States; that it is an extremely bad precedent and would very probably
                    raise similar ambitions in other malcontents. Previous conversations with the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs lead me to believe the Guatemalan Government views
                    the situation with concern and believes a stronger policy advisable, at least to
                    the extent of closing the Legation. The Minister even hinted that an economic
                    boycott would bring Martínez to terms in short order.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Donald</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
